 as one of the books very  gripping but chilling chapters will  here in the next edition of the  Academy's member magazine so I want to  thank dr. Davis for joining us this  evening we want to give special thanks  to the Perseus Books group for offering  copies of her book for sale here this  evening your purchase will go to support  the Academy will have time for a short  QA after the lecture if you'd come to  the microphone to ask your question  since we're all being recorded and then  we'll have a reception in book signing  so please stay to join us for that but  welcome Deborah Davis good evening the  honor is mine let me assure you i'm  really delighted to be here and this  fabulous building and to see so many  dear old friends and some new ones as  well i'm going to talk with you this  evening about the secret history the war  on cancer and i have to tell you a  little bit about where i come from to  set the context for that i grew up in a  town called Donora Pennsylvania how many  of you've ever heard of Donora  Pennsylvania this is a very unusually  well informed group at least ten percent  well I want to show you what it looked  like this is an extraordinary image that  was taken 1910 and it shows this huge  mill which was a six storeys tall that  blocked out the river and you see this  little thing right here this was a  bucket for the blast furnace it was put  out back because a man had fallen in and  been incinerated and when that happened  and it did with some regularity they  would bury the bucket out back his  remains presumably inside and the molten  material as it hardened people came to  Donora because smoke meant jobs and  that's what they wanted in those days we  would go outside and play and if you  blew your nose it would be black nobody  thought much of it well I only learned  when I went away to school that  something unusual had happened in an  aura in one five day period 20 people  dropped dead in Donora Pennsylvania in  nine  48 I came home one day and I said to my  mother mom was there another town with  the same name as ours she got kind of  quiet why are you asking i said well i  read in a book at school the town called  Donora was polluted now pollution  sounded like a dirty thing so i thought  had to be someplace else she said well  you remember how we used to wash the  walls down about once a month I was tall  so I got to help early on said yeah  remember how we used to drive with the  headlights on to it the afternoon  because it's kind of dark often I said  yeah well she said I guess today they  would call that pollution but back then  it was just a living that's when I  realized that I came from in a somewhat  unusual place and I wrote about what it  was like you see here the hills opposite  the zinc plant the plume there the smoke  coming out of their shows you what it  was like on a typical day that's not  snow on those hills nothing grew much on  them sometimes the graves would wash out  of the ground and we learned to live and  play in an environment like that in fact  it was great for a tomboy because when  you played kick the stick he kicked the  stick there you could slide to first  second and third base because there  wasn't much grass under your feet and  when you went outside as a kid you got  what they call the Donora measles sort  of black [ __ ] stuff all over the place  men would go to work with two white  shirts if they had a white-collar job  and by noon they'd have to change to the  second shirt because the first one would  be soiled this is what Pittsburgh looked  like in nineteen forty at noon if you  look carefully here you can see it's  daytime right but this is how dark it  was and that's what people expected was  normal this was the sign of prosperity  Pittsburgh became a leader in cleaning  up the air and what happened in Donora  in 1948 ultimately led to the Clean Air  Act to a recognition that if a large  quantity of pollution could kill some  people it killed 20 pe